new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b8d54da
--- /dev/null
+++ b/MIGRATION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# Moving The Daily Grind to a new origin
+
+Browsers tie an installed web app's identity (manifest `id`), its storage, and its
+OS-level shortcuts to the origin it was installed from. Nothing can move that record
+across origins, so the migration works in two parts:
+
+1. **User configuration** travels with the visitor. The old origin packages every
+   `localStorage` entry under the `dg:` prefix into the URL fragment of a redirect to
+   the new origin. Fragments are never sent to a server. The new origin imports the
+   entries (existing values there win) and remembers where they came from.
+2. **OS-level shortcuts** are redeclared, not copied. `manifest.json` ships the same
+   `shortcuts` list on both origins. Once the visitor reinstalls from the new origin,
+   the jump-list / long-press shortcuts reappear pointing at the new address. The new
+   origin shows a one-time "Reinstall app" banner (driven by `beforeinstallprompt`)
+   to make that a single click.
+
+## Cutover steps
+
+1. Edit `config.js`:
+   - `newOrigin`: the new origin, e.g. `"https://app.thedailygrind.coffee"`.
+   - `previousOrigins`: every old origin, e.g. `["https://www.dailygrind.example"]`.
+     Visitors arriving from a listed origin are imported silently; anyone else who
+     shows up with a payload in the URL is asked to confirm first.
+   - Optionally bump `cacheVersion`.
+2. Deploy the **same build to both origins**. The code picks its role from
+   `location.origin`:
+   - old origin → *sender*: redirects immediately, carrying the configuration.
+   - new origin → *receiver*: imports, strips the fragment, offers reinstall.
+3. Keep the old origin serving this build for as long as you want installed copies
+   to keep forwarding. The old install cannot be removed programmatically; the user
+   uninstalls it when they no longer need it.
+4. If you later add a Content-Security-Policy or `X-Frame-Options`, nothing here
+   needs an exception: the hand-off is a top-level navigation, not an iframe. (An
+   iframe + `postMessage` hand-off was deliberately avoided because third-party
+   storage partitioning would hide anything written in the frame.)
+
+## What counts as "user configuration"
+
+Any `localStorage` key starting with `storagePrefix` (`dg:`). New features that store
+preferences should use that prefix and they are migrated automatically. Payloads are
+capped at ~200 KB; for anything larger (IndexedDB, cookies, server-side state), move
+it server-side keyed by account instead of through the hand-off.
+
+## Verifying
+
+```
+npm test
+```
+
+runs the pure migration logic (`migration.js`) under `node:test`: role detection,
+payload encoding/decoding, import rules, and the end-to-end sender→receiver flow.
